Title: "Differentiation"
Id: "5c64e9bc-323f-470e-b56b-3812afd2a703"


Text: "Differentiation studies the first-order linear behavior of a function.
       The derivative at a point is not merely a symbolic quotient: it is the
       coefficient of the best linear approximation, with an error that is
       negligible relative to the increment. This viewpoint explains the chain
       rule, the mean value theorem, and the role of derivatives in local
       approximation."
Id: "e33f5272-051b-41b6-af7f-e193d1108154"


SectionTitle: "Differentiation"
Id: "d3e7ecb7-d361-4548-bbd1-4cf6cee3a2b5"


[\derivative:of{f}:at{x0}]
Defines: d is \real
when:
. x0 is \\anything
. f is \\anything
expresses:
. forAll: epsilon is \real
  where: epsilon \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
  then:
  . exists: delta is \real
    suchThat:
    . delta \:real.greater?:/ \real.zero
    . forAll: h is \real
      where:
      . h != \real.zero
      . \real.abs(h) \:real.less?:/ delta
      then: \real.abs(((f(x0 \.real.+./ h) \.real.-./ f(x0)) \.real.div./ h) \.real.-./ d) \:real.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. called: "derivative of $f?$ at $x0?$"
. written: "f?'(x0?)"
. description: "The derivative
                $f'(x_0) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x_0 + h) - f(x_0)}{h}$."
Id: "a8704cca-424a-4f17-a321-d5c3725e2090"


[\(differentiable)::real.function:on{U}]
Refines: f
when: U \:subset:/ \reals
specifies: f is \function:on{U}:to{\reals}
satisfies:
. forAll: x0 "in" U
  then:
  . exists: d is \real
    suchThat: d = \derivative:of{f}:at{x0}
Documented:
. adjective: "differentiable"
. description: "A function is differentiable on $U$ if its derivative exists at
                every point of $U$."
Id: "bddc1b83-707b-40f9-9b7f-c03513735f35"


SectionTitle: "The Mean Value Theorem"
Id: "20b85fa7-bbfb-4518-b953-1ee73f9b3eb3"


TextTheorem: "Mean value theorem. Let $a<b$. If $f:[a,b]\to\mathbb{R}$ is
              continuous on the closed interval and differentiable on the open
              interval $(a,b)$, then there exists $c\in(a,b)$ such that
              $f'(c)=(f(b)-f(a))/(b-a)$. Continuity at the endpoints and
              differentiability in the interior are separate hypotheses."
Id: "31c0c6c3-b04e-48c3-b6b3-37990aa819cd"


TextTheorem: "If $f'=0$ throughout an interval, then $f$ is constant there. If
              $f'\geq0$ throughout an interval, then $f$ is nondecreasing. Both
              conclusions follow by applying the mean value theorem to an
              arbitrary pair of points; the interval hypothesis ensures that the
              entire segment between them lies in the domain."
Id: "8f9c74de-06a2-4fcb-bee8-37af5b68b7eb"


TextTheorem: "Chain rule. If $f$ is differentiable at $x$ and $g$ is
              differentiable at $f(x)$, then $g\circ f$ is differentiable at $x$
              and $(g\circ f)'(x)=g'(f(x))f'(x)$. The statement expresses
              composition of the two best linear approximations; the error terms
              remain of smaller order than the original increment."
Id: "95dcd4db-216b-45d0-897f-a6a998c8da45"
